> > > Thank you for the response! While I understand the reason for why t y~x and y~~x should not be estimable, I'm confused as to why I *can* estimate it under some conditions. For example, the figure below summarizes a model with12 observed variables, where 4 latent factors are measured by 3 observed variables apiece. This model converges despite containing all possible covariances between the 4 latent factors AND two regressions relating the latent variables to each other: rs2 ~ rs1 and wr2 ~ wr1. This means that e.g. rs2 ~~ rs1 and rs2 ~ rs1 are both in the model syntax.
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > > On Friday, May 13, 2022 at 9:17:09 AM UTC-7 Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
> > > > >
> > > > > 1)  When regression equations with multiple predictors are included in the model (e.g. y ~ x + z), does specifying the variance of the endogenous variable (e.g. y ~~ 1*y) specify var(y) or the residual variance of y (i.e. y - x - z)?
> > > >
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > residual variances are model parameters, not marginal variances (except by coincidence with exogenous variables).
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > >
> > > > > 2) I noticed that it is possible to include both a regression coefficient (y ~ x) and an estimated covariance (y ~~ x) with sem(). Perhaps this is a substantive misunderstanding, but it's odd to me that one could estimate the regression parameter but omit the covariance parameter, which I assume fixes the value at 0. What equations are we specifying with(out) including the covariance of two factors when the model regresses one on the other?
> > > >
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > You can estimate either, but the model would fail to converge on a unique solution if you specify a model that estimates both.  It is up to the researcher to specify a model that is identified (e.g., never regress a variable on itself).
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > >
> > > > > 3) Should the regression formula always be used when temporal ordering (e.g. the same factor measured at different times) restricts the relationship between two factors to a unidirectional relationship?
> > > >
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > There is nothing in the matrix that says anything about time or "this is the same variable again."  It is up to the researcher to specify a model that corresponds to their theory and design.
